## Answer

**Yes**, the Riemann–Stieltjes integrability of $f^3$ implies that of $f$. Below is the proof.

---

### Proof

Let $\alpha$ be a monotonically increasing function on $[a,b]$ (the standard setting for Riemann–Stieltjes integration). Suppose $f^3 \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$ on $[a,b]$, i.e., $f^3$ is Riemann–Stieltjes integrable with respect to $\alpha$.

We use the following standard theorem on composition (see, e.g., Rudin, *Principles of Mathematical Analysis*, Theorem 6.11):

> **Composition Theorem.** If $g \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$ on $[a,b]$ and $\varphi$ is continuous on the range of $g$, then $\varphi \circ g \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$ on $[a,b]$.

Set $g = f^3$ and $\varphi(t) = \sqrt[3]{t}$, the real cube-root function.

**Key observation:** The real cube-root function $\varphi(t) = t^{1/3}$ is continuous on all of $\mathbb{R}$. In particular, it is continuous at $t = 0$ (unlike, say, $\sqrt{t}$, which is not even defined for $t < 0$ and is not Lipschitz at $0$). Continuity of $\varphi$ on all of $\mathbb{R}$ means it is automatically continuous on the range of $g = f^3$, whatever that range may be.

Since $g = f^3 \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$, $g$ is bounded, so its range is a bounded subset of $\mathbb{R}$. The function $\varphi$ is continuous on this range. By the Composition Theorem,

$$\varphi \circ g = \varphi \circ f^3 = f \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha).$$

Therefore $f$ is Riemann–Stieltjes integrable with respect to $\alpha$ on $[a,b]$. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark on why the cube is special.** The argument hinges on the fact that the inverse operation—taking the real cube root—is *continuous everywhere*. This is what distinguishes the cube from the square: if we only knew $f^2 \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$, we could not conclude $f \in \mathcal{R}(\alpha)$, because $\sqrt{t}$ is not continuous (or even real-valued) on the range of $f^2$ when $f^2$ takes the value $0$ and $f$ changes sign, and more fundamentally $f \mapsto f^2$ is not injective, so $f$ cannot be recovered as a continuous function of $f^2$. The real cube root, being a globally defined, everywhere-continuous inverse of the (bijective) cubing map $x \mapsto x^3$ on $\mathbb{R}$, avoids both obstructions.

### PROOF COMPLETE
